---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Section Summary: The Create Performance Task</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-4" />

        <p class="text-secondary">
          Activities 3.1 &ndash; 3.5. Use this to review, or to fill in a definition you missed.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">1.</span> What It Is and What the Rules Are</h2>

          <p>
            The task is scored on
            specific things, and some of the rules cost you everything if you
            break them. Know what counts as code you
            developed.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Student-developed</dt>
            <dd>Written by you, individually or with a partner. Calls to libraries and event handlers do not count.</dd>
            <dt>Event-driven programming</dt>
            <dd>Program statements run when triggered, rather than through the sequential flow of control.</dd>
            <dt>Personalized Project Reference</dt>
            <dd>Four code segments you take into the exam. Comments stripped.</dd>
            <dt>Attribution</dt>
            <dd>Naming the origin of code you did not write. Required, and cheap.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">2.</span> The Proposal</h2>

          <p>
            Decide what the program must do before you
            build it, and decide in advance what you will cut. Incremental and
            iterative are two different habits.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Specification</dt>
            <dd>A statement of the requirements for a program.</dd>
            <dt>Incremental development</dt>
            <dd>Breaking a problem into smaller pieces and making each one work before adding it to the whole.</dd>
            <dt>Iterative development</dt>
            <dd>Refining and revising based on feedback, testing, or reflection &mdash; including revisiting earlier phases.</dd>
            <dt>Investigation</dt>
            <dd>Finding out a program's requirements before designing it &mdash; surveys, interviews, user testing, observation.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">3.</span> The Build</h2>

          <p>
            Build in small pieces, each working before the
            next is added, and keep a record of the choices you made and what
            they did to the program.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Development journal</dt>
            <dd>A record of design choices and their effect on the program's function.</dd>
            <dt>Incremental</dt>
            <dd>Each piece works before the next is added. Your eight-line plan.</dd>
            <dt>Iterative</dt>
            <dd>Going back and revising an earlier decision because testing told you to.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">4.</span> The Personalized Project Reference</h2>

          <p>
            Four code segments go
            with you into the exam. Comments are stripped, so choose code you
            can talk about without them.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Program documentation</dt>
            <dd>A written description of what code does and how it was developed.</dd>
            <dt>Comment</dt>
            <dd>Documentation written into the program, read by people, with no effect on how the program runs.</dd>
            <dt>Submitted as final</dt>
            <dd>The state a component must reach to be scored. Cannot be revised afterward.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">5.</span> The Video, and Final Submission</h2>

          <p>
            The video has to show the
            program running, within tight constraints. Once a component is
            submitted as final it cannot be changed.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Program functionality</dt>
            <dd>The behavior of a program during execution &mdash; often described by how a user interacts with it.</dd>
            <dt>Attestation</dt>
            <dd>Your confirmation that you followed the guidelines and did not plagiarize.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  dl {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  dt {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  dd {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }
  section {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  code {
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
</style>
